  :adr/body "## What moved

`cloud.itonami.app.connectors` (ADR-2608097000) derives the default enabled
set from `historical-grant` so a connector added later can never silently
widen anybody's grant. Most of the file is mechanism the native word-typed
slice deliberately refuses — set membership (`contains?`), registry
traversal (`creg/providers` / `cp/descriptor` / `cm/tools`), the
client-overlay map, wire-string assembly, and the OAuth catalogue
descriptors. All of that stays host.

What moved is the one thing in the file that is a DECISION about plain
strings: the `scope-implications` table — the three places where one scope
already contains another, each verified against the provider's own
documentation rather than assumed from the name:

    gmail.readonly  inside gmail.modify
    Mail.Read       inside Mail.ReadWrite
    Mail.ReadBasic  inside Mail.ReadWrite

The cljc's `covered?` answered \"was `scope` inside `granted`, directly or by
implication?\" through `(get scope-implications scope)`. The implication
table IS that judgement, so the core expresses it on the native slice as a
total function of the scope name:

    (implier-of scope) -> the scope that would cover it, or none

The host prepares set membership — the one thing `covered?` needs that is
not on the slice (`contains?` on both sides of the implication) — and
routes on the core's verdict. A scope outside the table answers none, so
`when-let` reduces to the direct-membership check alone, exactly as the old
map lookup produced. Byte-faithful: the cljc's `(get scope-implications
scope)` IS this function.

Landed this tick:

- `src/cloud/itonami/app/connectors_core.kotoba` — `implier-of`, `:string`
  in, `[:option :string]` out (some of the covering scope, none otherwise);
  the three implications written out as `string=?` limbs, no map
- `kotoba_oracle.cljc` `cores` gains `:connectors-core`
- `connectors.clj` `covered?` routes through `kotoba-oracle`; the
  `scope-implications` map is gone (the core owns the table) and set
  membership / registry / catalogue machinery stays host
- parity test `test/cloud/itonami/app/connectors_kotoba_parity_test.cljk`
  + KIR regeneration + JVM-free gen_kir_amu identity

## The split is byte-faithful, not just behavior-preserving

The implication table is a total function of the scope name, and the cljc's
`(get scope-implications scope)` IS this table. The core invents nothing: a
scope outside the table answers none, so `covered?`'s `when-let` reduces to
the direct check. The existing `connectors_test.clj` (subset property,
default enabled set, enabled/also semantics, catalogue rows, widened
scopes, unknown-provider reporting) now runs through the oracle path and
pins it.

Nil discipline (ADR-2609081000): an unknown scope answers none — never a
throw and never a guessed implier. A guessed implier would silently widen a
grant; the core invents nothing.

## Measured bounds on the pinned verifier (amu bin, jvm-free)

- `amu check src/cloud/itonami/app/connectors_core.kotoba --jvm-free` →
  `:ok true, :effects #{}` (the authoritative gate; measured on the landed
  core)
- `if` / `string=?` with `:string` literals and an `[:option :string]`
  result (via `option-some-of` / `option-none-of`) → admitted (the option
  constructor is the same vocabulary the existing option-argument cores and
  their parity probes already use)
- `contains?` / set literals / registry traversal do NOT cross the slice —
  that is why the host keeps them

